\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSRulerMarker displays a symbol on an NSRulerView, indicating a location for whatever graphic element it represents in the client of the NSRulerView (for example, a margin or tab setting, or the edges of a graphic on the page). A ruler marker comprises three primary attributes: the image it displays on the NSRulerView, the location of that image, and the object it represents. The \b setImage:\b0 , \b setMarkerLocation:\b0  and \b setRepresentedObject:\b0  methods set each of these attributes, respectively. In addition, a ruler marker records an offset for the image, allowing it to be placed relative to the marker location much in the way a cursor\'27s hot spot relates a cursor image to the mouse location; the \b setImageOrigin:\b0  method establishes this offset.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Most of these attributes are set upon initialization by the \b initWithRulerView:markerLocation:image:imageOrigin:\b0  method. New ruler markers don\'27t have represented objects; the client typically establishes the represented object in its \b rulerView:didAddMarker:\b0  method. A new NSRulerMarker can be moved around in its NSRulerView, but not removed from it. The \b setMovable:\b0  and \b setRemovable:\b0  methods alter these default settings.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Represented objects allow the NSRulerView\'27s client to distinguish among different attributes of the selection. In the NSRulerView client methods, the client can retrieve the marker\'27s represented object to determine what attribute to alter. Generic attributes can be represented by string or other value objects, such as the edge names \'aaLeft\'ba, \'aaRight\'ba, \'aaTop\'ba, and \'aaBottom\'ba. Attributes already implemented as objects can be represented by those objects. For example, the OPENSTEP text system records tab stops as NSTextTab objects, which include the tab location and its alignment. When an NSTextView is the client view of a ruler, it simply makes the NSTextTabs the represented objects of the ruler markers.\
\fs14\
