\fi0 \b \i0 markerLocation:\b0 (float)\i location\
\fi0 \b \i0 image:\b0 (NSImage\'80*)\i anImage\
\fi0 \b \i0 imageOrigin:\b0 (NSPoint)\i imageOrigin\
\fs14\
\i0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Initializes a newly allocated NSRulerMarker object, associating it with (but not adding it to) \i aRulerView\i0  and assigning the attributes provided.\i  location\i0  is the \i x\i0  or \i y\i0  position of the marker in the client view\'27s coordinate system, depending on whether the NSRulerView is horizontal or vertical. \i anImage\i0  is the image displayed at the marker location, and \i imageOrigin\i0  is the point within the image that\'27s positioned at the marker location, expressed in pixels relative to the lower-left corner of the image. This method raises an NSInvalidArgumentException if \i aRulerView\i0  or \i anImage\i0  is \b nil\b0 .\
\fs14\
\pard \s46 \li2310 \fi-798 \ri1007 \ql \tx2308 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Note:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 The image used to draw the marker must be appropriate for the orientation of the ruler. Markers may need to look different on a horizontal ruler than on a vertical ruler, and the NSRulerView neither scales nor rotates the images.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 To add the new ruler marker to \i aRulerView\i0 , use either of NSRulerView\'27s \b addMarker:\b0  or \b trackMarker:withMouseEvent:\b0  methods. \b addMarker:\b0  immediately puts the marker on the ruler, while \b trackMarker:withMousEvent:\b0  allows the client view to intercede in the addition and placement of the marker.\
\fs14\
\fs28 A new ruler marker is can be moved on its NSRulerView, but not removed. Use \b setMovable:\b0  and \b setRemovable:\b0  to change these attributes. The new ruler marker also has no represented object; use \b setRepresentedObject:\b0  to provide or change it.\
\fs14\
\fs28 This method retains \i anImage\i0 , since it\'27s an essential part of the ruler marker, but doesn\'27t retain \i aRulerView\i0  (the NSRulerView instead retains the new marker when it\'27s added). This method is the designated initializer for the NSRulerMarker class. Returns \b self\b0 .\
\fs14\
