\fs14\
\i0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Handles user manipulation of the receiver in its NSRulerView. NSRulerView invokes this method automatically to add a new marker or to move or remove an existing marker. You should never need to invoke it directly.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If \i flag\i0  is YES, the receiver is a new marker being added to its NSRulerView. Before the receiver actually adds itself to the NSRulerView, it queries the NSRulerView\'27s client view using \b rulerView:shouldAddMarker:\b0 . If the client view responds to this method and returns NO, this method immediately returns NO and the new marker isn\'27t added.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If \i flag\i0  is NO, this method attempts to move or remove an existing marker, once again based on responses from the NSRulerView\'27s client view. If the receiver is neither movable nor removable, this method immediately returns NO. Further, if the NSRulerView\'27s client responds to \b rulerView:shouldMoveMarker:\b0  and returns NO, this method returns NO, indicating that the receiver can\'27t be moved.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If the receiver is being added or moved, this method queries the client view using \b rulerView:willAddMarker:atLocation: \b0 or \b rulerView:willMoveMarker:toLocation:\b0 , respectively. If the client responds to the method, the return value is used as the receiver\'27s location. These methods are invoked repeatedly as the receiver is dragged within the NSRulerView.\b \
\fs14\
\b0 \fs28 If the receiver is an existing marker being removed (dragged off the ruler), this method queries the client view using \b rulerView:shouldRemoveMarker:\b0 . If the client responds to this method and returns NO, the marker is pinned to the NSRulerView\'27s baseline (following the mouse on the baseline if it\'27s movable).\
\fs14\
\fs28 When the user releases the mouse, this method informs the client view of the marker\'27s new status using \b rulerView:didAddMarker:\b0 , \b rulerView:didMoveMarker:\b0 , or \b rulerView:didRemoveMarker:\b0  as appropriate. The client view can use this notification to set the marker\'27s represented object, modify its state and redisplay (for example, adjusting text layout around a new tab stop), or take whatever other action it might need. If \i flag\i0  is YES and the user dragged the new marker away from the ruler, the marker isn\'27t added, no message is sent, and this method returns NO.\
\fs14\
\fs28 See the NSRulerView class description for more information on these client methods.\
